    {
      "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-8",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "8 Definitions",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-8"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf, p.15",
      "values:originalText": "In this Act:\nACIC means the agency known as the Australian Criminal\nIntelligence Commission established by the Australian Crime\nCommission Act 2002.\naffected agency, in relation to a machinery of government change:\nsee section 73A.\nagency has the meaning given by section 71.\nallocation, in relation to a disclosure:\n\n(a) means the allocation of the handling of the disclosure under\nsection 43; and\n\n(b) includes the reallocation of the handling of the disclosure as\ndecided under section 45.\nExample: One situation in which a disclosure may be reallocated under\nsection 45 is in response to a recommendation by the\nOmbudsman or the IGIS under section 55.\nanother law or power means:\n\n(a) a law of the Commonwealth (including procedures under\nsuch a law), other than this Act; or\n\n(b) the executive power of the Commonwealth.\nAUSTRAC means the Australian Transaction Reports and Analysis\nCentre continued in existence by the Anti-Money Laundering and\nCounter-Terrorism Financing Act 2006.\nAustralia, when used in a geographical sense, includes the external\nTerritories.\nAustralian legal practitioner has the same meaning as in the\nEvidence Act 1995.",
      "skos:definition": "In this Act:\nACIC means the agency known as the Australian Criminal\nIntelligence Commission established by the Australian Crime\nCommission Act 2002.\naffected agency, in relation to a machinery of government change:\nsee section 73A.\nagency has the meaning given by section 71.\nallocation, in relation to a disclosure:\n\n(a) means the allocation of the handling of the disclosure under\nsection 43; and\n\n(b) includes the reallocation of the handling of the disclosure as\ndecided under section 45.\nExample: One situation in which a disclosure may be reallocated under\nsection 45 is in response to a recommendation by the\nOmbudsman or the IGIS under section 55.\nanother law or power means:\n\n(a) a law of the Commonwealth (including procedures under\nsuch a law), other than this Act; or\n\n(b) the executive power of the Commonwealth.\nAUSTRAC means the Australian Transaction Reports and Analysis\nCentre continued in existence by the Anti-Money Laundering and\nCounter-Terrorism Financing Act 2006.\nAustralia, when used in a geographical sense, includes the external\nTerritories.\nAustralian legal practitioner has the same meaning as in the\nEvidence Act 1995.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-8-norm"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f08a8bc85c0f6d2dbba5ea08aba9df5698f57e01fc428d22ca8578429846d5b0",
      "skos:note": "Definitions section"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-8",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "8 Definitions",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 15,
      "values:originalText": "In this Act:\nACIC means the agency known as the Australian Criminal\nIntelligence Commission established by the Australian Crime\nCommission Act 2002.\naffected agency, in relation to a machinery of government change:\nsee section 73A.\nagency has the meaning given by section 71.\nallocation, in relation to a disclosure:\n\n(a) means the allocation of the handling of the disclosure under\nsection 43; and\n\n(b) includes the reallocation of the handling of the disclosure as\ndecided under section 45.\nExample: One situation in which a disclosure may be reallocated under\nsection 45 is in response to a recommendation by the\nOmbudsman or the IGIS under section 55.\nanother law or power means:\n\n(a) a law of the Commonwealth (including procedures under\nsuch a law), other than this Act; or\n\n(b) the executive power of the Commonwealth.\nAUSTRAC means the Australian Transaction Reports and Analysis\nCentre continued in existence by the Anti-Money Laundering and\nCounter-Terrorism Financing Act 2006.\nAustralia, when used in a geographical sense, includes the external\nTerritories.\nAustralian legal practitioner has the same meaning as in the\nEvidence Act 1995."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-8-norm",
      "@type": "values:Undertaking",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-8"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-9",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "9 Simplified outline",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-9"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf, p.23",
      "values:originalText": "The following is a simplified outline of this Division:\nAn individual is not subject to any civil, criminal or administrative\nliability for making a public interest disclosure or providing\nassistance in relation to a public interest disclosure.\nIt is an offence to take a reprisal against a person because of a\npublic interest disclosure (including a proposed or a suspected\npublic interest disclosure).\nThe Federal Court or Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia\n(Division 2) may make orders for civil remedies (including\ncompensation, injunctions and reinstatement of employment) if a\nreprisal is taken against a person because of a public interest\ndisclosure (including a proposed or a suspected public interest\ndisclosure).\nIt is an offence to disclose the identity of an individual who makes\na public interest disclosure.\nNote 1:\nDivision 2 sets out the kinds of public interest disclosures.\nNote 2:\nThe principal officer and the authorised officers of an agency have a\nduty to protect a person who is, or has been, a public official\nbelonging to the agency from reprisals relating to a public interest\ndisclosure (see subsections 59(9) and 60(2)).",
      "skos:definition": "The following is a simplified outline of this Division:\nAn individual is not subject to any civil, criminal or administrative\nliability for making a public interest disclosure or providing\nassistance in relation to a public interest disclosure.\nIt is an offence to take a reprisal against a person because of a\npublic interest disclosure (including a proposed or a suspected\npublic interest disclosure).\nThe Federal Court or Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia\n(Division 2) may make orders for civil remedies (including\ncompensation, injunctions and reinstatement of employment) if a\nreprisal is taken against a person because of a public interest\ndisclosure (including a proposed or a suspected public interest\ndisclosure).\nIt is an offence to disclose the identity of an individual who makes\na public interest disclosure.\nNote 1:\nDivision 2 sets out the kinds of public interest disclosures.\nNote 2:\nThe principal officer and the authorised officers of an agency have a\nduty to protect a person who is, or has been, a public official\nbelonging to the agency from reprisals relating to a public interest\ndisclosure (see subsections 59(9) and 60(2)).",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-9-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-9-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "8020d7eb8c450a45b905b12341f587e18e136b6c0df63c7126d1d8093cf8b4ee",
      "skos:note": "Taking reprisal against a person for making a public interest disclosure is an offence."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-9",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "9 Simplified outline",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 23,
      "values:originalText": "The following is a simplified outline of this Division:\nAn individual is not subject to any civil, criminal or administrative\nliability for making a public interest disclosure or providing\nassistance in relation to a public interest disclosure.\nIt is an offence to take a reprisal against a person because of a\npublic interest disclosure (including a proposed or a suspected\npublic interest disclosure).\nThe Federal Court or Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia\n(Division 2) may make orders for civil remedies (including\ncompensation, injunctions and reinstatement of employment) if a\nreprisal is taken against a person because of a public interest\ndisclosure (including a proposed or a suspected public interest\ndisclosure).\nIt is an offence to disclose the identity of an individual who makes\na public interest disclosure.\nNote 1:\nDivision 2 sets out the kinds of public interest disclosures.\nNote 2:\nThe principal officer and the authorised officers of an agency have a\nduty to protect a person who is, or has been, a public official\nbelonging to the agency from reprisals relating to a public interest\ndisclosure (see subsections 59(9) and 60(2))."
    },
    {
