    {
      "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-29a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "29A Meaning of personal work-related conduct",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-29a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf, p.47",
      "values:originalText": "Personal work-related conduct is conduct (by act or omission)\nengaged in by a public official (the first official) in relation to\nanother public official (the second official) that:\n\n(a) occurs in relation to, or in the course of, either or both of the\nfollowing:\n\n(i) the second official’s engagement or appointment as a\npublic official;\n\n(ii) the second official’s employment, or exercise of\nfunctions and powers, as a public official; and\n\n(b) has, or would tend to have, personal implications for the\nsecond official.\nExamples: The following are examples of personal work-related conduct:\n(a) conduct relating to an interpersonal conflict between the first\nofficial and the second official (including, but not limited to,\nbullying or harassment);\n(b) conduct relating to the transfer or promotion of the second\nofficial;",
      "skos:definition": "Personal work-related conduct is conduct (by act or omission)\nengaged in by a public official (the first official) in relation to\nanother public official (the second official) that:\n\n(a) occurs in relation to, or in the course of, either or both of the\nfollowing:\n\n(i) the second official’s engagement or appointment as a\npublic official;\n\n(ii) the second official’s employment, or exercise of\nfunctions and powers, as a public official; and\n\n(b) has, or would tend to have, personal implications for the\nsecond official.\nExamples: The following are examples of personal work-related conduct:\n(a) conduct relating to an interpersonal conflict between the first\nofficial and the second official (including, but not limited to,\nbullying or harassment);\n(b) conduct relating to the transfer or promotion of the second\nofficial;",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-29a-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-29a-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-29a-logic-deontic-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-29a-logic-alleninterval-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-29a-logic-deontic-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-29a-logic-deontic-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "7b8dcfcc53a15f987d4291bd5515a4b3614e16e406d8d0a2e734182b58eec118",
      "skos:note": "Public officials must not engage in personal work-related conduct that has implications for another official."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-29a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "29A Meaning of personal work-related conduct",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 47,
      "values:originalText": "Personal work-related conduct is conduct (by act or omission)\nengaged in by a public official (the first official) in relation to\nanother public official (the second official) that:\n\n(a) occurs in relation to, or in the course of, either or both of the\nfollowing:\n\n(i) the second official’s engagement or appointment as a\npublic official;\n\n(ii) the second official’s employment, or exercise of\nfunctions and powers, as a public official; and\n\n(b) has, or would tend to have, personal implications for the\nsecond official.\nExamples: The following are examples of personal work-related conduct:\n(a) conduct relating to an interpersonal conflict between the first\nofficial and the second official (including, but not limited to,\nbullying or harassment);\n(b) conduct relating to the transfer or promotion of the second\nofficial;"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-29a-norm",
