    {
      "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-42",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "42 Simplified outline",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-42"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf, p.57",
      "values:originalText": "The following is a simplified outline of this Division:\nIf a disclosure is made to an authorised officer of an agency (either\ndirectly by the discloser or through a supervisor of the discloser),\nhe or she allocates the handling of the disclosure to one or more\nagencies.\nA stop action direction under the NACC Act may prevent the\nallocation of some or all of a disclosure.\nNote 1:\nIn order for a disclosure to be an internal disclosure (one of the types\nof public interest disclosure), the disclosure must be made to an\nauthorised officer or a supervisor.\nNote 2:\nThe way a disclosure is allocated (or a refusal to allocate a disclosure)\nmay be the subject of a complaint to the Ombudsman under the\nOmbudsman Act 1976, or (in the case of an intelligence agency, or\nAUSTRAC, the Department of Home Affairs or the Australian\nFederal Police in relation to that agency’s intelligence functions) to\nthe IGIS under the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security Act\n1986.",
      "skos:definition": "The following is a simplified outline of this Division:\nIf a disclosure is made to an authorised officer of an agency (either\ndirectly by the discloser or through a supervisor of the discloser),\nhe or she allocates the handling of the disclosure to one or more\nagencies.\nA stop action direction under the NACC Act may prevent the\nallocation of some or all of a disclosure.\nNote 1:\nIn order for a disclosure to be an internal disclosure (one of the types\nof public interest disclosure), the disclosure must be made to an\nauthorised officer or a supervisor.\nNote 2:\nThe way a disclosure is allocated (or a refusal to allocate a disclosure)\nmay be the subject of a complaint to the Ombudsman under the\nOmbudsman Act 1976, or (in the case of an intelligence agency, or\nAUSTRAC, the Department of Home Affairs or the Australian\nFederal Police in relation to that agency’s intelligence functions) to\nthe IGIS under the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security Act\n1986.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-42-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-42-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "aff74a91b82aedfa2fb908620dc207a235e32a016d3fab84d2cdf6c5813f78d4",
      "skos:note": "Disclosure must be made to an authorised officer or a supervisor for it to be considered internal."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-42",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "42 Simplified outline",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 57,
      "values:originalText": "The following is a simplified outline of this Division:\nIf a disclosure is made to an authorised officer of an agency (either\ndirectly by the discloser or through a supervisor of the discloser),\nhe or she allocates the handling of the disclosure to one or more\nagencies.\nA stop action direction under the NACC Act may prevent the\nallocation of some or all of a disclosure.\nNote 1:\nIn order for a disclosure to be an internal disclosure (one of the types\nof public interest disclosure), the disclosure must be made to an\nauthorised officer or a supervisor.\nNote 2:\nThe way a disclosure is allocated (or a refusal to allocate a disclosure)\nmay be the subject of a complaint to the Ombudsman under the\nOmbudsman Act 1976, or (in the case of an intelligence agency, or\nAUSTRAC, the Department of Home Affairs or the Australian\nFederal Police in relation to that agency’s intelligence functions) to\nthe IGIS under the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security Act\n1986."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-42-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
