      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-49",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "49 Investigative agency using separate investigative powers",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-49"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "public-interest-disclosure-act-2013.pdf, p.71",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Despite section 47, if the agency is an investigative agency that has\na separate investigative power in relation to the disclosure, the\nprincipal officer of the agency may decide:\n\n(a) to investigate the disclosure under that separate investigative\npower; and\n\n(b) not to investigate the disclosure, or (if the investigation has\nstarted) not to investigate the disclosure further, under this\nAct.\n\n(2) A separate investigative power, in relation to the disclosure, is a\npower that an investigative agency has, otherwise than under this\nAct, to investigate the disclosure.\nNote 1:\nUnder the Ombudsman Act 1976, the Ombudsman has power to\ninvestigate a disclosure allocated to the Ombudsman.\nNote 2:\nUnder the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security Act 1986,\nthe IGIS has power to investigate a disclosure allocated to the IGIS.\n\n(3) On completing its investigation of the disclosure under its separate\ninvestigative power, the investigative agency must inform:\n\n(a) the principal officers of each of the agencies to which any of\nthe conduct disclosed relates; and\n\n(b) the discloser;\nthat the investigation is complete.\n\n(4) Paragraph (3)(b) does not apply if contacting the discloser is not\nreasonably practicable.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Despite section 47, if the agency is an investigative agency that has\na separate investigative power in relation to the disclosure, the\nprincipal officer of the agency may decide:\n\n(a) to investigate the disclosure under that separate investigative\npower; and\n\n(b) not to investigate the disclosure, or (if the investigation has\nstarted) not to investigate the disclosure further, under this\nAct.\n\n(2) A separate investigative power, in relation to the disclosure, is a\npower that an investigative agency has, otherwise than under this\nAct, to investigate the disclosure.\nNote 1:\nUnder the Ombudsman Act 1976, the Ombudsman has power to\ninvestigate a disclosure allocated to the Ombudsman.\nNote 2:\nUnder the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security Act 1986,\nthe IGIS has power to investigate a disclosure allocated to the IGIS.\n\n(3) On completing its investigation of the disclosure under its separate\ninvestigative power, the investigative agency must inform:\n\n(a) the principal officers of each of the agencies to which any of\nthe conduct disclosed relates; and\n\n(b) the discloser;\nthat the investigation is complete.\n\n(4) Paragraph (3)(b) does not apply if contacting the discloser is not\nreasonably practicable.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-49-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-49-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-49-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:public-interest-disclosure-act-2013-sec-49-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "ab304dafcec7b68ed958b1a87bc2f80217f30f21cd5df49f9b0540569db165eb"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/public-interest-disclosure-act-2013#sec-49",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "49 Investigative agency using separate investigative powers",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 71,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Despite section 47, if the agency is an investigative agency that has\na separate investigative power in relation to the disclosure, the\nprincipal officer of the agency may decide:\n\n(a) to investigate the disclosure under that separate investigative\npower; and\n\n(b) not to investigate the disclosure, or (if the investigation has\nstarted) not to investigate the disclosure further, under this\nAct.\n\n(2) A separate investigative power, in relation to the disclosure, is a\npower that an investigative agency has, otherwise than under this\nAct, to investigate the disclosure.\nNote 1:\nUnder the Ombudsman Act 1976, the Ombudsman has power to\ninvestigate a disclosure allocated to the Ombudsman.\nNote 2:\nUnder the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security Act 1986,\nthe IGIS has power to investigate a disclosure allocated to the IGIS.\n\n(3) On completing its investigation of the disclosure under its separate\ninvestigative power, the investigative agency must inform:\n\n(a) the principal officers of each of the agencies to which any of\nthe conduct disclosed relates; and\n\n(b) the discloser;\nthat the investigation is complete.\n\n(4) Paragraph (3)(b) does not apply if contacting the discloser is not\nreasonably practicable."
    },
